.TH AX25 3
.SH NAME
ax25 \- AX.25 over a packet device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind '#a' /net/ax25
.PP
.B /net/ax25/ctl
.br
.B /net/ax25/addr
.br
.B /net/ax25/stats
.br
.B /net/ax25/clone
.br
.BI /net/ax25/ n /ctl
.br
.BI /net/ax25/ n /data
.br
.BI /net/ax25/ n /local
.br
.BI /net/ax25/ n /remote
.br
.BI /net/ax25/ n /status
.br
.BI /net/ax25/ n /listen
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I ax25
device carries AX.25 frames over anything that delimits packets,
attached by a file descriptor written to its
.BR ctl ,
the way
.I ssl (3)
and
.I lora (3)
attach.
.PP
It is beside
.I lora (3)
rather than inside it.  A RadioHead address is a byte a station picks;
an AX.25 address is a callsign, and on an amateur band an unidentified
transmission is not permitted, so a satellite on 70cm has to say who it
is in a form a regulator and a receiving station both already read.  The
other half is that
.IR direwolf ,
a TNC and a handheld with a KISS port all decode AX.25 today and none
of them has heard of RadioHead.  The argument in full is in
.BR doc/ax25-plan.ms .
.PP
What goes on the air is an AX.25 frame without the HDLC flags, the bit
stuffing or the FCS \- the LoRa PHY already delimits the packet and
checks it, and a second CRC inside the first is paying twice:
.PP
.EX
	dest[7] src[7] [digi[7] ...] control[1] pid[1] info...
.EE
.PP
Each address is six bytes of callsign, ASCII, shifted left one bit and
padded with spaces, then an SSID byte holding the command or
has-been-repeated bit, two reserved bits sent as ones, four bits of
SSID, and the end-of-address bit on the last address only.
.PP
.B Ctl
takes:
.TP
.BI fd " n"
Attach the packet device open on file descriptor
.IR n .
Once only, and for the life of the boot.
.TP
.BI addr " CALL\fP-\fIn"
This station's callsign, at most six letters and digits, with an
optional SSID of 0 to 15.
.PP
Opening
.B clone
makes a conversation and returns its
.BR ctl ,
whose first read is the conversation's number; holding that descriptor
open holds the conversation open.  A conversation's
.B ctl
takes:
.TP
.BI connect " CALL\fP-\fIn\fP [via \fICALL\fP-\fIn\fP[,\fICALL\fP-\fIn\fP]]"
Send to that station, by that path, and take frames from it.
.TP
.B announce
Take frames from any station no other conversation has claimed.
.TP
.BI via " CALL\fP-\fIn\fP[,\fICALL\fP-\fIn\fP]"
The digipeater path on its own, at most two, which is what AX.25 2.2
allows.
.TP
.B disconnect
DISC, and wait for the answer.
.TP
.BI pid " n"
The protocol identifier byte; 0xf0, no layer 3, by default.
.TP
.BI ui " onPorB off"
Connectionless.  Off by default, so
.B connect
means what it says.  The mode cannot change under a connection.
.TP
.BI window " k"
Frames outstanding, 1 to 7, modulo 8.  Four by default, which is what
the specification suggests.
.TP
.BI n2 " n"
Tries before the link is declared gone.
.TP
.BI t1 " ms"
The retransmission timer.
.TP
.BI t2 " ms"
How long to wait before acknowledging.
.TP
.BI t3 " ms"
How long a silent link may stay silent before it is checked.
.TP
.BI busy " onPorB off"
Say RNR, and RR to release it.
.PP
.B Connect
sends SABM and does not return until the other end has answered or N2
tries have gone unanswered.  A connect that returned early would let
the next write fail for a reason belonging to this one.
.PP
Opening
.B listen
on an announced conversation waits for somebody to send a SABM, and
returns that connection's
.B ctl
\- a different conversation, whose number is its first read.  That is
.I ip (3)'s
shape, and it is what makes the protocol expressible: a connection is
per peer, so a second station calling one server has to become a second
conversation.
.I Lora (3)
has
.B announce
and no
.BR listen ,
and carries that as a bug.
.PP
.B T2
is not a refinement.  A station that has just been sent a burst is
very likely still transmitting the rest of it, so an acknowledgement
sent the instant a frame arrives lands on a deaf receiver and is lost:
measured, before this timer existed, three I frames went out, three RRs
came back, the sender heard none of them and retransmitted to N2.  With
T2 at a second, ten frames through a window of four cost the sender
eleven transmissions and four receptions \- one UA and three RRs \-
with no retransmissions at all, because one RR after a burst
acknowledges the whole burst.
.PP
.B T1
is a number this cannot compute.
.I Lora (3)
found that a station which has just transmitted cannot answer until it
has served its duty cycle, so a timeout shorter than that debt expires
before the reply can go out; working it out means knowing the modem's
parameters, which is the coupling this layering exists to avoid.  So T1
is set, and
.B status
reports it.
.PP
.B Data
is one information field per read and one per write, inheriting both of
.IR radio (3)'s
refusals.  In connected mode a write waits for room in the window
rather than failing, which is what a file interface says instead of
EWOULDBLOCK, and a read returns 0 when the link goes down.
.B QST
and
.B CQ
are destinations no station answers to, which is the whole of what
broadcast means here: there is no bit for it.
.PP
The
.B iounit
of
.B data
is the packet device's less the header this conversation's path needs:
239 with no digipeaters over a 255-byte packet device, and 225 with
two.  It is exact rather than a worst case, which is why
.B via
is refused while
.B data
is open \-
.B appl/lib/ssl.b
once opened
.B data
before pushing its descriptor and so published a budget computed from
nothing, and that is the mistake this ordering avoids.
.PP
.B N1 ,
whose specified default is 256, is therefore not a tuning parameter on
this link but a property of it, and it is this number.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/inferno/radio/devax25.c
.SH BUGS
SREJ, SABME and XID are not implemented, and neither is the 2.2
segmenter.  All four are optional in 2.2 and negotiated or absent, so a
station that never offers them is never sent one; the argument is in
.BR doc/ax25-plan.ms .
FRMR is not sent either, which 2.2 prefers.
.PP
This does not digipeat.  A frame with a hop still to go is counted
under
.B todigi
and dropped, separately from a frame for another station, because the
two mean different things about a link.
.PP
Four conversations, which is a constant.
.PP
Nothing here identifies the station periodically.  Most jurisdictions
require identification at an interval as well as on each transmission,
and every frame does carry the source callsign, but nothing sends a
beacon on its own.
.PP
.B #L
and
.B #a
cannot both hold one radio, because
.IR radio (3)'s
receive path is a claim.  That is correct and is also the only way to
find out, since neither device knows the other exists.
